    "created_at": "2025-12-02T01:02:37Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-02T01:02:37Z",
    "body": "> [@mfreed](https://github.com/mfreed) Right, but given that devs would still run into these exceptional cases the end result is the same user experience but now it's manually (and likely worse) implemented in JS which is the kind of thing the popover API was made to avoid.\n\nWell, right - you can do anything with Javascript. But the platform tries not to make it easy to build things that aren't good for users, to discourage it. It's good if users know that they can always click anywhere outside a popover to dismiss it, and that behavior is consistent across (most) websites.\n\n\n> What if there were an easy way to opt in to the light dismiss logic of the browser? I didn't dive too deep into the CloseWatcher API, is that (the browser's internal CloseWatcher that is) something that could be exposed? Or maybe a `closedby` attribute similar to `<dialog>`.\n\n[CloseWatcher](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CloseWatcher) is likely not what you're looking for. That's about hooking into the default \"close\" button (ESC on a keyboard, back button on a mobile phone, etc.). And `closedby` adds the same kind of light dismiss to `<dialog>`; i.e. you can't \"poke holes\" in the light dismiss clickable area there either.\n\nSorry, I think for your application, Javascript is actually the right solution. \n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
